WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.668
-Our first guest tonight
is an Oscar-, Emmy-,

00:00:02.668 --> 00:00:04.270
and Tony-nominated actress
you know from "Up in the Air,"

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:06.806
the "Pitch Perfect" movies,
and HBO Max's "Love Life."

00:00:06.806 --> 00:00:08.741
She stars in the film
"Alice, Darling,"

00:00:08.741 --> 00:00:11.277
which opens exclusively
in AMC theaters nationwide

00:00:11.277 --> 00:00:13.212
on January 20th.
Please welcome back to the show

00:00:13.212 --> 00:00:15.348
our friend Anna Kendrick,
everyone.

00:00:15.348 --> 00:00:16.849
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:16.849 --> 00:00:26.859 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:26.859 --> 00:00:28.227 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:28.227 --> 00:00:30.496
Welcome!

00:00:30.496 --> 00:00:33.499
-Thanks for having me back.
-I'm so happy to have you back.

00:00:33.499 --> 00:00:36.135
I want to ask about the movie
and a lot of other things,

00:00:36.135 --> 00:00:38.004
but I especially want to ask,
before we get started --

00:00:38.004 --> 00:00:40.840
you just did Sean Evans' show,
"Hot Ones."

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:42.142
-Yesterday.
-Yesterday?

00:00:42.142 --> 00:00:43.509
-Yes.
-So, you're still

00:00:43.509 --> 00:00:45.511
in the recovery period.
-I might still be on Neptune.

00:00:45.511 --> 00:00:47.313
-Yeah.
-I might watch this back

00:00:47.313 --> 00:00:49.582
and be like, "That happened?
I went on the show?"

00:00:49.582 --> 00:00:51.884
-So, I did it, as well.
I think this is the best photo

00:00:51.884 --> 00:00:53.486
of how I was feeling
when I did it.

00:00:53.486 --> 00:00:54.787
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:54.787 --> 00:00:57.624
-Was it -- how remarkable
did you find the experience?

00:00:57.624 --> 00:01:00.126
-Yeah, life altering
is what it was.

00:01:00.126 --> 00:01:03.262
Yeah, and I know that part of
the fun of watching the show

00:01:03.262 --> 00:01:05.665
is sort of watching people
in distress.

00:01:05.665 --> 00:01:07.300
And I will say,
I loved your episode.

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:08.300
-Thank you.

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:09.769
-Because you are suffering
so much.

00:01:09.769 --> 00:01:10.970
-I'm so --
[ Laughter ]

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:12.138
-You are really
going through it.

00:01:12.138 --> 00:01:14.507
-So upset the whole time.
-Yes.

00:01:14.507 --> 00:01:17.276
And I think that I got mine
is --

00:01:17.276 --> 00:01:18.912
like, yeah,
karma's a bitch, Anna.

00:01:18.912 --> 00:01:22.381
Because I suffered so much.

00:01:22.381 --> 00:01:24.016
-It's sort of
a remarkable construction,

00:01:24.016 --> 00:01:26.586
because I did find
I was answering questions

00:01:26.586 --> 00:01:28.221
probably in a way
that I would not have.

00:01:28.221 --> 00:01:30.456
-Yes!
I think I was like using words

00:01:30.456 --> 00:01:32.658
that I don't normally use.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:32.658 --> 00:01:34.494
I was like,
"Can I speak French?

00:01:34.494 --> 00:01:35.895
Like, if I just tried,
would it work?"

00:01:35.895 --> 00:01:37.396
[ Laughter ]

00:01:37.396 --> 00:01:39.565
-Well, there you go.
I'm very excited to see that.

00:01:39.565 --> 00:01:42.035
I'm very excited about the fact
that you just finished

00:01:42.035 --> 00:01:43.635
directing your first film.
-Yeah.

00:01:43.635 --> 00:01:45.438
-How did you like
the experience?

00:01:45.438 --> 00:01:46.939
-Thank you, thank you,
thank you!

00:01:46.939 --> 00:01:49.442
It really, like -- it kind of
came out of nowhere.

00:01:49.442 --> 00:01:51.243
And I had the time of my life
doing it.

00:01:51.243 --> 00:01:53.813
It was so much fun.
I loved it so much.

00:01:53.813 --> 00:01:55.581
It was also, like,
the first time

00:01:55.581 --> 00:01:58.918
that I've dealt with the budget
in a real way.

00:01:58.918 --> 00:02:02.221
I kind of didn't know
that the -- when --

00:02:02.221 --> 00:02:04.556
even when you're the director,
it's kind of like weird

00:02:04.556 --> 00:02:07.226
used-car-lot rules
where no one will tell you,

00:02:07.226 --> 00:02:09.328
like, a hard number.
-Uh-huh.

00:02:09.328 --> 00:02:11.697
-So, I started to, like,
read facial expressions.

00:02:11.697 --> 00:02:13.933
Like, when I would ask for
something, it would be like,

00:02:13.933 --> 00:02:17.971
"Ooh, was that like a wince that
means $200 or like 5 grand?

00:02:17.971 --> 00:02:19.805
What are we talking about here?"

00:02:19.805 --> 00:02:21.574
And like --
sometimes I would be like,

00:02:21.574 --> 00:02:24.477
"If I'd known that 'that'
meant 10 grand,

00:02:24.477 --> 00:02:27.046
I wouldn't have asked for it!"
Like, cancel it, cancel it.

00:02:27.046 --> 00:02:29.348
It's fine."
-Sometimes -- I remember once

00:02:29.348 --> 00:02:30.650
there was something
where we were like,

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:32.418
"We would love to get a possum."

00:02:32.418 --> 00:02:34.954
And they were like,
"Please get a raccoon."

00:02:34.954 --> 00:02:36.556
[ Laughter ]

00:02:36.556 --> 00:02:38.457
And that's how we found out,
like, possums,

00:02:38.457 --> 00:02:39.792
you gotta
pay through the nose for.

00:02:39.792 --> 00:02:40.993
But a raccoon --
-Really?

00:02:40.993 --> 00:02:42.528
-Yeah.
Raccoon, you just put, like,

00:02:42.528 --> 00:02:44.263
a garbage can out,
and they all just show up.

00:02:44.263 --> 00:02:45.765
-They'll just do --
-They'll just do it, yeah.

00:02:45.765 --> 00:02:48.201
-They're easy,
but possums are like divas.

00:02:48.201 --> 00:02:49.334
They're like,
"I'm not getting out of bed

00:02:49.334 --> 00:02:50.837
for less than 20 grand."
-Oh, yeah.

00:02:50.837 --> 00:02:52.238
[ Laughter ]

00:02:52.238 --> 00:02:53.706
"No, thank you."
-Wow.

00:02:53.706 --> 00:02:55.842
-So that, I know what you mean.
Like, it's very --

00:02:55.842 --> 00:02:58.811
Now, this is a true story.
Were you aware of the story

00:02:58.811 --> 00:03:00.312
before you read the script
about it?

00:03:00.312 --> 00:03:02.681
-Yeah, I sort of knew the story.
So the story is --

00:03:02.681 --> 00:03:04.284
it's, yeah,
based on this crazy thing

00:03:04.284 --> 00:03:06.285
that happened in the 1970s.

00:03:06.285 --> 00:03:08.154
A woman
went on "The Dating Game,"

00:03:08.154 --> 00:03:09.388
that game show
"The Dating Game."

00:03:09.388 --> 00:03:11.256
And the person
she ended up picking

00:03:11.256 --> 00:03:12.925
turned out to be
a serial killer.

00:03:12.925 --> 00:03:15.161
[ Audience groans ]
Very cool.

00:03:15.161 --> 00:03:16.495
[ Laughter ]

00:03:16.495 --> 00:03:20.499
So, it's just this, like, yeah,
completely insane story,

00:03:20.499 --> 00:03:23.135
and it's, like, kind of -- like,
it so captures your imagination

00:03:23.135 --> 00:03:26.272
because it's like,
"Who am I on a date with?"

00:03:26.272 --> 00:03:27.373
You know?
[ Laughter ]

00:03:27.373 --> 00:03:29.475
Like, it could kind of be
anybody.

00:03:29.475 --> 00:03:31.510
-Have you ever in your life
had a moment where you were

00:03:31.510 --> 00:03:34.880
dating someone where you
thought, "This is a red flag"?

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:36.849
-When I was like 21
or something,

00:03:36.849 --> 00:03:39.752
I went on a date with a guy.
We'd met at a party,

00:03:39.752 --> 00:03:41.654
and we talked on the phone
a little bit.

00:03:41.654 --> 00:03:43.656
And I went on a first date
with this guy.

00:03:43.656 --> 00:03:47.760
And at the end of the date,
he -- he, like, seemed very shy,

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:50.863
and then he handed me
a piece of paper,

00:03:50.863 --> 00:03:53.999
and it was a poem about me.
-Okay.

00:03:53.999 --> 00:03:55.667
-Which is a big move
for a first date.

00:03:55.667 --> 00:03:58.170
-Yeah.
-A really intense move.

00:03:58.170 --> 00:04:01.373
And it -- like, on the one hand,
I was like,

00:04:01.373 --> 00:04:02.508
"Maybe that's sweet."

00:04:02.508 --> 00:04:05.778
But he'd done that thing
that you do, like,

00:04:05.778 --> 00:04:08.747
if you're doing, like,
an elementary school project

00:04:08.747 --> 00:04:10.249
about pirates or something

00:04:10.249 --> 00:04:12.318
where he, like,
burned the edges of the paper.

00:04:12.318 --> 00:04:14.520
[ Laughter ]

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:16.756
To make it look, like,
old timey.

00:04:16.756 --> 00:04:18.691
[ Laughter ]

00:04:18.691 --> 00:04:20.059
And that should've been
a red flag.

00:04:20.059 --> 00:04:21.761
-Yeah.
So, was he saying, like,

00:04:21.761 --> 00:04:24.530
"I wrote this about you
in a past life"?

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:26.131
-Yeah, maybe.

00:04:26.131 --> 00:04:28.334
Yeah, we've been together
through the ages or something.

00:04:28.334 --> 00:04:32.738
I did spend the next like three
months breaking up with him.

00:04:32.738 --> 00:04:34.540
-Yeah.
-Like, I didn't just, like,

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:36.409
ghost him.
And I probably should have.

00:04:36.409 --> 00:04:38.144
Sometimes people
need to get ghosted.

00:04:38.144 --> 00:04:39.612
-Yeah.
-'Cause I was just, like,

00:04:39.612 --> 00:04:42.714
trying to break it slowly to him
for, like, a really long time

00:04:42.714 --> 00:04:44.683
that I -- no, thank you.
-You should have given him

00:04:44.683 --> 00:04:47.653
one of those burned edges, like,
"I found the treasure map."

00:04:47.653 --> 00:04:49.888
-"My past self
also rejected you."

00:04:49.888 --> 00:04:51.257
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:51.257 --> 00:04:54.560
-Now, I have heard that you
did not watch old episodes

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:56.028
of "The Dating Game" growing up.

00:04:56.028 --> 00:04:57.563
Like, I think sometimes
I watched them on TV.

00:04:57.563 --> 00:04:59.065
But you have a favorite
dating show

00:04:59.065 --> 00:05:00.699
that I think is my favorite,
as well.

00:05:00.699 --> 00:05:03.135
-Yeah. So, I'm obsessed
with "Love is Blind."

00:05:03.135 --> 00:05:05.170
-"Love is Blind" is a special --
[ Cheers ]

00:05:05.170 --> 00:05:06.506
-It's all I want to talk about.

00:05:06.506 --> 00:05:08.040
Like, I don't want to keep
doing this interview.

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:09.608
Whoever just whoo'd,
I want to talk to you

00:05:09.608 --> 00:05:11.244
about "Love is Blind."
[ Laughter ]

00:05:11.244 --> 00:05:13.546
That's, like, all --
-Now, I have something to admit.

00:05:13.546 --> 00:05:14.680
I only watched the first season.

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:16.282
Was the second season
as exceptional?

00:05:16.282 --> 00:05:17.717
-This second season
was wonderful.

00:05:17.717 --> 00:05:20.320
This last season, season three,
is really something, Seth.

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:21.654
-Okay.
-You really got to get on it.

00:05:21.654 --> 00:05:23.322
-I don't know why we didn't
get back on the horse.

00:05:23.322 --> 00:05:25.424
This sounds like
a real solid review.

00:05:25.424 --> 00:05:27.926
-I'm, like, jealous of you
that you get to experience

00:05:27.926 --> 00:05:30.196
season two and three.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:30.196 --> 00:05:32.364
-I'm very excited for that.
I have so much more to ask you.

00:05:32.364 --> 00:05:35.234
We'll be right back with more
from Anna Kendrick, everyone.

